   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第九 （敦賀）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 9 — Tsurumaki (鶴牧藩) tail and Tsuruga (敦賀藩) opening (shared leaf)",
   "table_number": 9,
   "page_topic": "The tail of Tsurumaki's officials/military register (junior councillors + 120 troops) followed by the ○敦賀藩 boundary marker opening Tsuruga's councillor roster.",
   "page_summary_en": "This fascicle 9 (officials and military) leaf sits across a domain boundary marked by the ○ before 敦賀藩 (Tsuruga). Because the columns read right to left, the material to the RIGHT of that ○marker belongs to the PRECEDING domain, Tsurumaki (鶴牧藩): its junior-councillor counts (少参事 1, 権少参事 5), six named officers, and its military figure of 120 infantry (兵隊). The ○敦賀藩 marker then opens Tsuruga, whose councillor columns follow to the LEFT — Senior Councillor (大参事) Teramachi Shirōzaemon, two Provisional Senior Councillors (権大参事), and several junior councillors — along with a two-man liaison office (公用役掛) and a short editorial note at lower left."
  },
  {
   "id": "nagoya_kokudaka",
   "sequence": 43,
   "domain_canonical": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_en": "Nagoya (Owari)",
   "image": "images/upper_p156_left.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 156,
   "page_side": "L",
   "book_page": "二百八十七",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四 (名古屋)",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nagoya (名古屋藩): land assessment and sub-fiefs",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "kokudaka — reformed survey, sub-fief highs, and the 8-bu tax note",
   "page_summary_en": "This leaf opens the fascicle 4 (civil and economic) entry for Nagoya (名古屋藩), the senior Tokugawa shinpan of the Owari-Tokugawa house, with lands scattered across Owari, Mino, Ōmi and Settsu. Reflecting its reformed survey of June 1870, it records a current 高 (kokudaka) of 853,125 koku — among the largest in the country — from which sundry deductions (諸引高) of 40,736 leave a residual (殘高) of 778,897, with 261,250 koku delivered as tax (租納高) and a further surveyed 'external figure' (外數) of 282,150 koku. The page also itemises Owari's semi-autonomous sub-fiefs as separate managed-yield (所務高) entries, including Ishikawa Tahachirō's 19,171 koku and 10,000 koku returned by the Imao domain, and carries the 此八分出 ('eight-tenths output') note explaining how the tax-rice was recalculated."
  },
  {
   "id": "nagoya_tax_area",
   "sequence": 44,
   "domain_canonical": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_en": "Nagoya (Owari)",
   "image": "images/upper_p157_right.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 157,
   "page_side": "R",
   "book_page": "",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四 （名古屋）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nagoya (名古屋藩): tax and cultivated area",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "Nagoya (Owari) tax and cultivated-area register: koku deductions, net assessed/levied yield, and total/exempt/waste arable area continuing the kokudaka entry.",
   "page_summary_en": "This leaf continues the fascicle 4 (civil and economic) entry for Nagoya (名古屋藩), the Owari-Tokugawa domain, carrying its assessment forward from the preceding leaf. Reading right to left it gives a koku block — sundry deductions (諸引高) of 766, a remaining yield (残高) of 9,234, tax paid (租納) of 2,599, and a measured 'eight-tenths output' quantity of 2,807 — followed by an area section (內) recording total arable land (反別) of 11,164 chō drawn from holdings in Owari, Mino, Mikawa, Ōmi and Shinano, of which 1,288 chō is exempt or transferred land (引地) and 8,565 chō remains taxable (残反別), with a final area tax (税納) of 8,969 koku. This is a single-domain leaf, with no ○ boundary marker."
  },
  {
   "id": "nagoya_kokudaka_demographics",
   "sequence": 45,
   "domain_canonical": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_en": "Nagoya (Owari)",
   "image": "images/upper_p157_left.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 157,
   "page_side": "L",
   "book_page": "",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四（名古屋）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nagoya (名古屋藩): kokudaka and population",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "Nagoya (Owari) civil register: tail of the kokudaka/rice-revenue block (including the 山村・千村・毛利 and 石河 sub-fief administered-kokudaka figures) plus the household and population (士族/卒, with 男/女 splits) block.",
   "page_summary_en": "This is Nagoya domain's (the Owari-Tokugawa house) civil and economic register in the fourth fascicle of the survey. It completes the land and rice-revenue figures — a combined current rice yield (合現米) of 320,938 koku, plus additional administered kokudaka held by the Kiso sub-fief stewards (13,485 koku) and by Ishikawa Tahachirō (石河太八郎, 10,097 koku) whose internal breakdown the source states was not reported. It then gives the household and population block: 8,650 samurai-class households (戸數), of which 2,530 are 士族 (samurai households) and 6,120 are 卒 (lower-samurai), for a population (人員) of 49,340 — 15,644 shizoku (7,629 male / 8,015 female) and 33,696 sotsu (17,844 male / 15,852 female). The whole leaf is Nagoya's continuing register; no neighboring domain begins here."
  },
  {
   "id": "nagoya_demographics",
   "sequence": 46,
   "domain_canonical": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_en": "Nagoya (Owari)",
   "image": "images/upper_p158_right.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 158,
   "page_side": "R",
   "book_page": "二百九十",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四 (名古屋)",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nagoya (名古屋藩): commoner and religious population",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "population by class — household counts paired with class populations (with M/F)",
   "page_summary_en": "This page continues Nagoya domain's (Owari) demographic register in the fourth fascicle, pairing household and institution counts with the matching class populations. It records 192,177 commoner households (平民戸數), of which most are farming households, alongside 2,438 Buddhist temples (寺院), 811 shrine-priest households (社家), 518 outcaste (穢多) households, and 1,531 hinin and beggar households (非人乞丐). Each population is given with a male/female split — for example an internal population (内人員) of 840,896 (420,055 male / 420,841 female), 5,663 Buddhist clergy (内僧, including 86 nuns), and 6,001 hinin and beggars (2,873 male / 3,128 female). Unusually for these registers, Owari recorded its commoners in full detail; the domain's second population total and its military figures print on the facing half-page."
  },
  {
   "id": "nagoya_officials",
   "sequence": 47,
   "domain_canonical": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "名古屋藩",
   "domain_en": "Nagoya (Owari)",
   "image": "images/lower_p193_right.jpg",
   "volume": "vol2",
   "pdf_page": 193,
   "page_side": "R",
   "book_page": "",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第九（鶴田、名古屋）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 9 — Tsuruta (鶴田藩) and Nagoya (名古屋藩): officials and military",
   "table_number": 9,
   "page_topic": "Officials and military (Nagoya; Tsuruta tail)",
   "page_summary_en": "This is an officials-and-military leaf from the ninth fascicle, and it carries two domains across a boundary. To the right of the ○名古屋藩 domain-boundary marker is the tail of the previous domain, Tsuruta (鶴田藩, Hizen), with its own ○marker, four councillor rank headings, and its named appointees stacked in columns. To the left of the marker is Nagoya's own record: its officials are given only as headcounts — 2 senior councillors (大参事), 6 junior-senior councillors (権大参事), 5 junior councillors (少参事), and 11 provisional junior councillors (権少参事) — followed by the military block (兵隊, troops): 2,480 samurai-class soldiers (士族兵) and 4,720 lower-samurai soldiers (卒族兵), for a total of 7,200. The leftmost Tsuruta name-column overflows to the right of the Nagoya marker but still belongs to Tsuruta."
  },
  {
   "id": "nakatsu_boundary",
   "sequence": 48,
   "domain_canonical": "中津藩",
   "domain_en": "Nakatsu",
   "domain_hi_name": "中津藩",
   "image": "images/upper_p158_left.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 158,
   "page_side": "L",
   "book_page": "",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四（中津）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nagoya (名古屋藩) tail and Nakatsu (中津藩) opening",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "Domain-boundary page: the tail of Nagoya's commoner-population block (right of the ○中津藩 marker) meets the head of Nakatsu's kokudaka / tax-yield block (left of the marker).",
   "page_summary_en": "This is a boundary page in the fourth fascicle (the civil and economic register), read right to left. The right-hand columns finish the previous domain, Nagoya (名古屋藩): a population (人員) of 724,569 (360,899 male / 363,670 female), 28,634 merchant households, 488 temple halls with 731 clergy, 88 yin-yang diviner (陰陽師) households, and a standing force of 7,200 men. The ○中津藩 domain-boundary marker, with its territory note spanning districts of Buzen, Bungo, and Chikuzen, then opens Nakatsu domain (the Okudaira house). Nakatsu's figures begin on the left: an assessed yield (草高) of 100,000 koku, a tax yield (物成高) of 54,354 koku of rice plus 238 ryō of gold, and a main land tax (正租) of 46,310 koku of rice."
  },
  {
   "id": "nakatsu_taxes_demographics",
   "sequence": 49,
   "domain_canonical": "中津藩",
   "domain_hi_name": "中津藩",
   "domain_en": "Nakatsu",
   "image": "images/upper_p159_right.jpg",
   "volume": "vol1",
   "pdf_page": 159,
   "page_side": "R",
   "book_page": "",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覧表第四 （中津）",
   "page_header_en": "Domain Survey (藩制一覧表), Fascicle 4 — Nakatsu (中津藩): taxes and population",
   "table_number": 4,
   "page_topic": "Nakatsu domain's civil register: miscellaneous tax (money + rice) figures, household and population totals, and the religious-house / clergy breakdown.",
   "page_summary_en": "This page continues Nakatsu domain's (中津藩, the Okudaira house of Buzen) civil register in the fourth fascicle. The right side carries the miscellaneous-tax group (雜税) — a large cash sum of some 20,279 ryō, a rice figure of 294 koku, and further cash sums. The center gives 20,767 households (戸數) and a population (人員) of 98,542, with an itemized breakdown: 1,523 samurai, lower-samurai, and sundry households (士卒雜人共) totaling 7,133 people (4,055 male / 3,078 female); 170 shrine-priest households (社家); 227 Buddhist temples (寺院); and 287 shrines (神社). The entire leaf is Nakatsu; the next domain's boundary marker would begin on the following page."
  },
  {
   "id": "nakatsu_officials_military",
   "sequence": 50,
   "domain_canonical": "中津藩",
