   "page_side": "L",
   "book_page": "八十三",
   "page_header_kanji": "藩制一覽表第二（富山）",
   "page_header_en": "Han Institutions Survey (Hansei ichiran), Fascicle 2 — running head names Toyama",
   "table_number": 2,
   "page_topic": "Close of the Toyotsu entry and opening of the Toyama entry: shrines, districts, troops, kokudaka and the five-year average land tax",
   "page_summary_en": "The left half of PDF page 54 (printed page 83) of fascicle 2 straddles two domains. The six columns to the right of the ○富山藩 marker close out the preceding Toyotsu (豐津藩) entry, whose marker stands on the previous page: 2,227 shrines (248 principal, 163 auxiliary, 1,816 branch — the three sub-counts sum exactly to the total), five districts, an unrecorded village count, no warship return, a governor's household of five, and roughly 2,700 troops. Left of the marker the Toyama entry begins with a kusadaka of 145,637.801 koku, given as the figure remaining after flood-damaged land was deducted, and a five-year average land tax of 64,616.25 koku of rice plus 20,494 kan 972 mon in cash, alongside miscellaneous tax cash of 15,171 ryō 2 bu. Two interlinear notes carry the underlying arithmetic: 10,040.242 koku of Nei district kusadaka commuted to cash payment, leaving 9,895.119 koku after a 145.121 koku flood deduction; and a gross kusadaka of 149,257.629 koku over the whole of Nei district (368 villages) plus 119 villages of Niikawa district, less 3,620.548 koku of flood-damaged and altered land."
  },
